\name{TailcatServer}
\alias{TailcatServer}
\title{Tailcat server}
\description{
A tailcat server registers with a public relay and publishes a secret, shareable address. Clients holding the address can dial the ports the server listens on, over TCP or UDP, without accounts or port forwarding.

Blocking methods have an \verb{_async()} counterpart that returns a \code{\link[promises:promise]{promises::promise()}} resolving with the same value, or rejecting with the same condition. Asynchronous results are delivered by the later event loop, so they only arrive while R is idle at the console, inside Shiny or httpuv, or while you call \code{\link[later:run_now]{later::run_now()}}. The result goes to the loop that was current when the operation started, so an operation started inside \code{\link[later:with_temp_loop]{later::with_temp_loop()}} or \code{\link[later:with_loop]{later::with_loop()}} completes while that loop runs. If that loop is destroyed before the operation completes, the result is delivered on the global loop instead (checked about once a second while such operations are pending). A pending asynchronous operation can be canceled with \code{\link[=tailcat_cancel]{tailcat_cancel()}}.

Blocking methods can be interrupted (Ctrl-C or Esc), which cancels the operation. If the operation completes at the same moment, its result is returned and that interrupt is dropped rather than losing data such as bytes just read.

The address and the private and pre-shared keys are secrets: anyone with the address can connect. Printing a server never shows them. \verb{$address} and the \code{address} element of \verb{$info()} are \link{tailcat_secret} strings, which print as \verb{<redacted>} but otherwise behave like the full address (for example with \code{writeLines()} or \code{as.character()}). The server does not keep the address in an R field, so saving the server object (for example with \code{saveRDS()} or \code{save.image()}) does not write the address to disk. Handles do not survive serialization: a server restored from a saved session is closed.

Once a server is closed, every method other than \verb{$close()}, \verb{$format()}, and \verb{$print()}, and every field other than \verb{$is_closed}, raises a \code{tailcat_closed} error; nothing is kept from before.
}
\section{Timeouts}{

Every blocking method and function of this package takes a \code{timeout} in seconds. \code{Inf} waits indefinitely. \code{timeout = 0} is a non-blocking poll: the call returns what is available right away (for example data already received, or a connection already waiting) and otherwise raises \code{tailcat_timeout} at once. This differs from tailcat's C ABI (\href{https://github.com/tailscale/tailcat/blob/e00cdde6ff787f801361ff85ed085775c775d4c5/cmd/libtailcat/tailcat.h}{tailcat.h}), where a zero timeout is an already expired deadline that fails without trying; rtailcat gives a poll a deadline of a few milliseconds instead. A poll waits for an operation started before it in the same direction of a connection only for that short time.

A timeout ends only that call: the server, listener, client, or connection stays usable.
}

\section{Errors}{

Errors are conditions with class \code{tailcat_error} and one subclass that says what went wrong. Each also carries an integer \code{status} (the status code of the C ABI) and \code{bytes}, the number of bytes transferred before the error (for reads and writes; otherwise \code{0}). The message starts with a one-line summary; a line starting \verb{tailcat:} gives tailcat's own description when it has one.\tabular{lll}{
   \code{status} \tab Class \tab Meaning \cr
   1 \tab \code{tailcat_invalid_argument} \tab An argument is invalid \cr
   2 \tab \code{tailcat_closed} \tab The object, or the client or server that owns it, is closed \cr
   3 \tab \code{tailcat_timeout} \tab The timeout ran out \cr
   4 \tab \code{tailcat_canceled} \tab The operation was canceled with \code{\link[=tailcat_cancel]{tailcat_cancel()}} \cr
   5 \tab \code{tailcat_failure} \tab Anything else, including calls made in the wrong state \cr
}


Branch on the class (for example with \code{tryCatch(..., tailcat_timeout = function(e) ...)}) or the \code{status}, not on the message, whose wording may change.
}

\section{Operations in flight}{

Every operation that can block (each method or function with a \code{timeout}, and fields such as \verb{$address} that ask tailcat), synchronous or asynchronous, holds its own operating system thread from the moment it starts until it ends, including while it waits its turn behind other reads or writes on a connection. The option \code{rtailcat.max_operations} caps how many such operations may be in flight in the R process at once. It must be a single positive whole number, or \code{Inf} for no cap, and defaults to 1024, which stays well below the per-process thread limits of macOS (about 6,000) and typical Linux systems and the 10,000 threads at which the Go runtime ends the process. The option is read each time an operation starts. An operation started when the cap is reached fails at once with a \code{tailcat_failure} error that names the option and gives the limit, without affecting the operations already in flight; once some of them finish or are canceled, new operations work again. If the option is set to an invalid value, every such operation fails with a \code{tailcat_failure} error saying so.
}

